catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ 07 — one script, three modes: production, dry run, audit.
\
\ The capstone, and the one that is about utility rather than about a design
\ claim. Every real deployment script grows a `--dry-run` flag, and the flag
\ leaks: an `if dry_run` at every step, checked by nobody, and a code path
\ that is only exercised when someone remembers to test it.
\
\ Here the script has no flag and no branch. It says WHAT to do; a handler
\ chosen at the call says what doing it means. The dry run is not a mode of
\ the script — it is a different meaning for the same script.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- The vocabulary of the job, as an effect. Nothing here is an
\ implementation; these are the things a deployment is allowed to do.
catcat> effect Deploy {
    declare fetch   ( str -- str )
    declare install ( str -- )
    declare restart ( str -- )
}
effect Deploy fetch install restart
catcat> \ --- The script. This is the whole program, and it is the same text in all
\ three modes below. Its type says exactly what it can touch: `!Deploy`, and
\ nothing else. It cannot print, cannot call C, cannot fail.
catcat> define one ( $svc:str -- !Deploy ) { $svc fetch install $svc restart }
defined one ( str -- !Deploy )
catcat> define script ( -- !Deploy ) { "web" one "db" one "cache" one }
defined script ( -- !Deploy )
catcat> \ Unhandled, it does nothing at all — there is no default meaning:
catcat> script
unhandled: fetch escaped with no handler in scope
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Mode 1 — production
\ ======================================================================
catcat> define production ( -- !IO ) {
  handle Deploy over ( ) init { } {
    fetch   { "  fetching " print dup print "\n" print "/pkg/" swap cat }
    install { "  installing " print print "\n" print }
    restart { "  restarting " print print "\n" print }
  } { script }
}
defined production ( -- !IO )
catcat> "[production]\n" print production
[production]
  fetching web
  installing /pkg/web
  restarting web
  fetching db
  installing /pkg/db
  restarting db
  fetching cache
  installing /pkg/cache
  restarting cache
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Mode 2 — dry run
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ Same script. The handler narrates instead of acting, and `fetch` still has
\ to produce a plausible artifact name, because the script's TYPE says it
\ does. A dry run that returns nothing would not typecheck, which is the
\ property a boolean flag cannot give you.
catcat> define dry_run ( -- !IO ) {
  handle Deploy over ( ) init { } {
    fetch   { "  would fetch " print dup print "\n" print "/pkg/" swap cat }
    install { "  would install " print print "\n" print }
    restart { "  would restart " print print "\n" print }
  } { script }
}
defined dry_run ( -- !IO )
catcat> "[dry run]\n" print dry_run
[dry run]
  would fetch web
  would install /pkg/web
  would restart web
  would fetch db
  would install /pkg/db
  would restart db
  would fetch cache
  would install /pkg/cache
  would restart cache
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Mode 3 — audit
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ Same script again, and this handler does no IO whatsoever: it folds the run
\ into a counter. So `audit` is a PURE FUNCTION — look at the signature the
\ REPL reports. The deployment script, run, with no effects in its type.
\
\ That is what makes it usable as a test: it returns a value you can compare.
catcat> define audit ( -- i64 ) {
  handle Deploy over ( i64 ) init { 0 } {
    fetch   { swap "/pkg/" swap cat swap 1 + }
    install { swap pop 1 + }
    restart { swap pop 1 + }
  } { script }
}
defined audit ( -- i64 )
catcat> "[audit] steps: " print audit show print "\n" print
[audit] steps: 9
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Why this is not dependency injection
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ Nothing was injected. `script` takes no handler parameter, holds no
\ reference to one, and was compiled before any of the three existed. The
\ handler is not a value it received; it is a frame the CALLER installed.
\
\ And the choice is not all-or-nothing. A handler is a set of implementations,
\ one per operation, so a single step can be stubbed while the rest runs for
\ real — the thing a `--dry-run` boolean can never express, because there is
\ only one of it:
catcat> define mixed ( -- !IO ) {
  handle Deploy over ( ) init { } {
    fetch   { "  fetching " print dup print "\n" print "/pkg/" swap cat }
    install { "  installing " print print "\n" print }
    restart { "  SKIPPED restart of " print print "\n" print }
  } { script }
}
defined mixed ( -- !IO )
catcat> "[install for real, skip restarts]\n" print mixed
[install for real, skip restarts]
  fetching web
  installing /pkg/web
  SKIPPED restart of web
  fetching db
  installing /pkg/db
  SKIPPED restart of db
  fetching cache
  installing /pkg/cache
  SKIPPED restart of cache
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ What this demo cannot do yet
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ The service list is three literals in `script` rather than a collection,
\ because there are no arrays and no lists. That is the ceiling the roadmap
\ (NOTES/N04_Roadmap.md §4.1) puts first, and it is the only thing standing
\ between this and a script someone would actually run.
